WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:02.440
MARTIN OMANDER: Hi, Veer.

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.240
VEER MUCHANDI: Hello, Martin.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:06.730
MARTIN OMANDER: So you are
an App Modernization Solution

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:08.230
Manager at Google, right?

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:11.740
And you have an app for
us to modernize today?

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:14.140
VEER MUCHANDI: Yes, we have
a Legacy two-tier Node.js

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.750
application running on a VM.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:20.560
And we want to modernize it
using serverless technologies.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.100
The VM is costing
us money 24 by 7.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.400
Also, we have to assign someone
with the right operation skills

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.990
to maintain it.

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.507
We'd like Google
to do that for us.

00:00:31.507 --> 00:00:32.590
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:36.527
And does the team running
this app know containers?

00:00:36.527 --> 00:00:38.860
VEER MUCHANDI: The team has
started the containerization

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:39.580
journey.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:42.850
They considered Kubernetes,
but they don't have

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.617
enough experience with that.

00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:47.200
MARTIN OMANDER: A
lot of organizations

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.610
are in that place.

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.170
Let's migrate the
app for them using

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:53.800
Cloud Run, which doesn't require
them to learn Kubernetes.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.380
VEER MUCHANDI: This is
what the app UI looks like.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:09.200
It's a simple to-do
list application.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.270
Let me add a couple
of to-do list items

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:14.690
about modernizing
this application.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.070
MARTIN OMANDER: Got it.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:18.660
What does the
architecture look like?

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.020
VEER MUCHANDI: Here
it is, a web frontend,

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:25.160
and Node.js backend,
and a MongoDB database.

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:28.190
The HTML and JavaScript
for web frontend

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:31.570
is served by the
Node.js backend.

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:34.000
MARTIN OMANDER: Does the
app use server-side sessions

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.970
or any other storage
outside the database?

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:37.720
VEER MUCHANDI: No.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:41.650
It's a simple app where all
the users see the same content.

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:44.000
All the data is in the database.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.390
Let's keep the
database as is for now.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:49.960
We'll move the Node.js backend
and the serving of HTML

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.510
to Cloud Run, then we
can shut down that VM.

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.310
MARTIN OMANDER:
That makes sense.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.430
Let's look at the database
machine for a minute.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:00.430
VEER MUCHANDI: We need
to set up a serverless VP

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:03.910
connector from Cloud
Run to this database.

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:05.020
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:08.259
Let me click the Serverless
VPC connector option here.

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:10.120
And then I'll create one.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:15.780
I'll give it a name, and I'll
use all the default settings.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.318
And now let's create it.

00:02:18.318 --> 00:02:19.860
This will take a
minute, so let's get

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.170
started on the next step.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:25.050
VEER MUCHANDI: Let's have a
look at the source code here.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.540
This is a Git repo,
which is good.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.000
Most of the enterprises
want their code protected,

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:35.100
especially when the development
is done offshore or remotely.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.630
Think COVID, right?

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:40.650
I'm going to use a browser,
browser-based editor that

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.150
works like VS Code but doesn't
depend on my workstation.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.470
MARTIN OMANDER: Good idea.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.870
Editing the code in the
cloud also means that it

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.240
doesn't matter what
version of Node.js--

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.110
NPM, et cetera-- that you
or I have on our laptops.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:56.130
VEER MUCHANDI: Right.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.380
First, I'll open the
Google Cloud shell here,

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:02.400
which gives me a command
line in the cloud.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.920
Now that I have
a command line, I

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.727
can run a Git
clone of this repo.

00:03:08.727 --> 00:03:09.810
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.880
And then you'll edit
it in the cloud?

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.990
VEER MUCHANDI: Yeah, I'll click
here to start the cloud editor.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.090
This editor looks
and feels similar

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:23.140
to a traditional editor, like
VS code running on my laptop,

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.397
but it runs in the cloud.

00:03:25.397 --> 00:03:26.480
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.820
The Cloud Run docs
say that our code

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.970
should listen on a port
defined by the port environment

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:33.267
variable?

00:03:33.267 --> 00:03:34.100
VEER MUCHANDI: Yeah.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:35.030
Here it is.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:38.450
It looks like the
existing code already

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.780
does that so we don't
have to change anything.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:46.510
If the port had been hardcoded
here, we have to change it.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:47.740
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.480
How does the code connect
to the database, Veer?

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:51.880
VEER MUCHANDI: Well, let's see.

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:55.420
It looks like it reads
the connection parameters

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:59.020
from the environment
variables, as well.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:02.590
Whoever wrote this application
knew what they were doing.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.290
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah, well done.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:07.900
Veer, do you think we
should write a Docker file

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.210
and build a container
ourselves for this app?

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:12.160
VEER MUCHANDI: Well, we could.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.690
It's not very hard.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:16.160
But the team isn't very
comfortable with containers

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:16.660
yet.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:22.385
So let's deploy from source
code and use Buildpacks instead.

00:04:22.385 --> 00:04:24.010
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah,
that makes sense.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:29.490
It's a Node app so we should
probably run NPM install first.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.280
VEER MUCHANDI: OK,
I'm doing so now.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.640
Now that all the packages
have been installed,

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:40.170
I will prep the gcloud
run deploy command.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:41.400
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.310
We need to tell Cloud Run
about the VPC connector

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.497
and the environment variables.

00:04:46.497 --> 00:04:47.580
VEER MUCHANDI: Absolutely.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:52.080
We can also set all that
in Google Cloud console,

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.060
but the team wants things
done from the command line

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:57.330
so they can automate it later.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.900
Here is the gcloud run command
with all the environment

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:04.690
variables, with database
connection parameters.

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:08.026
I'm also adding
the VPC connector.

00:05:08.026 --> 00:05:11.670
MARTIN OMANDER: Hmm, very nice.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:13.050
VEER MUCHANDI: Let's run it.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:16.560
It's asking me a few
follow-up questions here

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.020
about region and such.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.192
I'll answer them.

00:05:21.192 --> 00:05:22.275
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:05:22.275 --> 00:05:25.200
It looks like the
build is running.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.620
Let me open the build log
here in the Cloud Console.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:29.460
Uh-huh.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:32.220
Yeah, it looks like
things are happening here.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.530
The Buildpack is
taking our source code

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:36.930
and building a container
from it without us

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.210
having to create a Docker
file or know anything

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:40.530
about containers.

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.390
That makes it easy
for developers.

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:45.960
VEER MUCHANDI: It looks
like the build is done now.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.202
Look at the container
is replaced.

00:05:49.202 --> 00:05:51.390
MARTIN OMANDER: Uh-huh.

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:53.950
Ah, there is our container.

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:56.450
VEER MUCHANDI: Now let's go to
the Cloud Run in the Console.

00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:58.160
MARTIN OMANDER: Let's see.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:00.830
Yeah, there's our
deployed service.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:02.970
The URL is shown here.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:04.500
Let's hit it.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.190
VEER MUCHANDI:
Hmm, look at that.

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:08.660
The app is working.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:12.380
And we are seeing the same
to-do list items in the UI

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:16.560
as before, when this app was
running in the virtual machine.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.880
And now I can mark these
two to-do list items

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.730
that I created before as done.

00:06:22.730 --> 00:06:23.780
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:06:23.780 --> 00:06:27.680
That reminds me, we don't
really need that virtual machine

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:28.910
anymore, do we?

00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:30.080
VEER MUCHANDI: We don't.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:30.947
Let's turn it off.

00:06:30.947 --> 00:06:32.030
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:35.090
I'm navigating to
Virtual Machines.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:39.140
There it is, and
I'm deleting it.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:40.910
VEER MUCHANDI: Feels
good, doesn't it?

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:42.890
We moved code from
a virtual machine

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:46.670
that was costing us money
24 by 7 to Cloud Run

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:49.730
where we pay for
CPU and memory usage

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:52.550
only when the request
is being served.

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:55.520
If we get less traffic
at night, we pay less.

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:57.530
And if we get no
traffic on weekends,

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:00.383
we don't pay anything at all.

00:07:00.383 --> 00:07:01.300
MARTIN OMANDER: Right.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:04.150
And even if the container
is ready to serve requests,

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:08.470
we don't pay anything until
an actual request comes in.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:10.150
Also, we do not have
to assign anyone

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:12.640
to maintain the virtual machine.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.440
Veer, will the team build
new versions of this web app?

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.190
VEER MUCHANDI: Yeah,
I think they probably

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:20.230
will need a CI/CD
pipeline for that,

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:22.810
but that will be a
story for another day.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:24.320
MARTIN OMANDER:
Sounds good to me.

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:25.780
Thank you for
watching, everyone.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:28.270
If you have any
questions for Veer or me,

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:30.020
let us know in the comments.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:32.620
Also, let us know what you
thought of today's episode

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:34.120
and other serverless
topics you'd

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:35.680
like to see in the future.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:36.940
Until next time.

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:39.990
